\name{Instrument}
\alias{Instrument}
\title{One instrument in UBI's unified instrument universe}
\description{
An instrument is looked up once, when it is created, through UBI's \verb{/api/instruments/details} route, and keeps only its identity, lot size and tick size. Every candle, quote and price after that is read from UBI at the moment it is asked for, with no caching here, because UBI runs on the same machine and caches in its own Redis.

\code{Instrument} inherits every analysis class, from \code{PriceStatistics} to \code{PerformanceMeasures}, so each of the analysis methods is available on every instrument and fetches its own candles through \code{prices()}.

Code normally creates one of the family classes, such as \code{Equity} or \code{EquityIndexOption}, rather than this class, because the family class asks only for the fields that identify one of its own contracts.

The examples below start with a short tour of the class, then show its properties and the functions on its class generator, in this order:
\itemize{
\item For \code{Instrument$shared_unified_broker_interface()}, show that every instrument built without a client of its own shares the one client, and ask it whether the session is connected.
\item For \code{Instrument$shared_unified_broker_interface()}, send a raw request to a UBI route that has no method of its own, here the last price of Infosys, through the shared client.
\item For \code{Instrument$shared_unified_broker_interface()}, hand the shared client to an instrument explicitly, as code that manages its own clients would.
\item For \code{quote}, print the headline fields of the full quote for Infosys.
\item For \code{quote}, check whether the quote is stale before trusting it, which UBI reports in the quote itself.
\item For \code{last_price}, print the last traded price of Infosys.
\item For \code{last_price}, print the last price of three indices side by side.
\item For \code{last_price}, value a hypothetical holding of 25 Infosys shares at the last price.
\item For \code{ohlc}, print the day's range of the Nifty index so far.
\item For \code{ohlc}, say whether Infosys opened with a gap up or a gap down against the previous close.
}
}
